1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,680
 The final thermoplastic we're going to look at this week is polystyrene,

2
00:00:03,680 --> 00:00:06,960
 which accounts for around 4% of global plastic production.

3
00:00:06,960 --> 00:00:11,360
 As its name suggests, polystyrene is polymerized from the styrene monomer.

4
00:00:11,360 --> 00:00:14,240
 This monomer is probably the most complicated of all the ones that we've seen before,

5
00:00:14,240 --> 00:00:17,680
 but thankfully we don't need to know anything about it because we're not chemists.

6
00:00:17,680 --> 00:00:20,000
 What I will say is that like the other monomers we've met,

7
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:22,880
 we've replaced one in 400 atoms with something different.

8
00:00:22,880 --> 00:00:25,040
 In this case, we're the benzene ring.

9
00:00:25,040 --> 00:00:28,080
 We produce polystyrene in a bunch of different form factors,

10
00:00:28,480 --> 00:00:33,680
 but probably the most common one is as an expanded polystyrene foam styrofoam.

11
00:00:33,680 --> 00:00:38,080
 Styrofoam is an incredibly useful and versatile material owing to its incredibly low

12
00:00:38,080 --> 00:00:42,080
 effective density. This makes it perfect for applications where you want to fill a space

13
00:00:42,080 --> 00:00:46,400
 without necessarily adding a lot of weight. For this exact reason, we commonly use styrofoam

14
00:00:46,400 --> 00:00:49,360
 to fill out boxes packaging when we're sending things places.

15
00:00:49,360 --> 00:00:53,760
 And it's also commonly used as a lightweight and cheap thermal insulator in houses.

16
00:00:53,760 --> 00:00:56,400
 Actually, and I can't pass this up while we're talking about it,

17
00:00:56,400 --> 00:01:00,000
 the fact that it's such a good thermal insulator coupled with the fact that it's food safe

18
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:03,600
 means that polystyrene foam can be used as a takeaway food container.

19
00:01:03,600 --> 00:01:05,680
 It's what you get your wholeile snack pack in.

20
00:01:05,680 --> 00:01:08,400
 What you may not have known is that you can also produce polystyrene

21
00:01:08,400 --> 00:01:11,440
 as a hard, brittle, solid, clear plastic.

22
00:01:11,440 --> 00:01:13,840
 It's what CD jewel cases are made out of.

23
00:01:13,840 --> 00:01:18,240
 While it is very versatile, polystyrene, particularly when it's an expanded foam,

24
00:01:18,240 --> 00:01:22,480
 is not very recyclable at all. Because of its chemistry, you need enormous amounts of

25
00:01:22,480 --> 00:01:26,000
 of energy to effectively melt down and recycle polystyrene,

26
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:28,720
 and you're releasing pretty nasty fumes in the process.

27
00:01:28,720 --> 00:01:32,080
 Polystyrene foam also causes huge headaches for recycling plants,

28
00:01:32,080 --> 00:01:36,160
 because it has a tendency to get broken up and distributed amongst all of your other trash,

29
00:01:36,160 --> 00:01:40,880
 contaminating all of the other plastics that would otherwise be reasonably easy to recycle.

30
00:01:40,880 --> 00:01:45,040
 Long story short, probably don't put polystyrene foam in your recycling bin.

31
00:01:45,040 --> 00:01:48,560
 And with that, we've lightly touched on the foremost common thermoplastics.

32
00:01:48,560 --> 00:01:51,680
 There's some more information about these on the textbook page that accompanies

33
00:01:51,680 --> 00:01:55,360
 the last couple of videos, and you're of course encouraged to a bit of your own research.

34
00:01:55,360 --> 00:01:57,840
 Have a look at what edge back has to say about these plastics.

35
00:01:57,840 --> 00:02:00,800
 Compare them to the properties of other plastics that you might know.

36
00:02:00,800 --> 00:02:04,640
 And I'll finish up by saying that we'll talk about a couple of other types of thermoplastic,

37
00:02:04,640 --> 00:02:09,360
 the ones that you tend to get as 3D printing filaments next week when we talk about 3D printing.
